1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,621
At the end of August 2026,

2
00:00:02,621 --> 00:00:10,459
a Court of Arbitration said India's reasons were not enough to suspend a treaty it had put on hold sixteen months earlier.

3
00:00:10,459 --> 00:00:12,944
India rejected the award the same day.

4
00:00:12,944 --> 00:00:15,429
The treaty is the Indus Waters Treaty.

5
00:00:15,429 --> 00:00:17,641
Why does it matter for India?

6
00:00:23,867 --> 00:00:31,918
The Indus Waters Treaty was signed in Karachi in September 1960 by Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru and Pakistan's President Ayub Khan,

7
00:00:31,918 --> 00:00:35,797
after about nine years of talks brokered by the World Bank.

8
00:00:41,767 --> 00:00:47,290
It survived the wars of 1965 and 1971, and the Kargil conflict of 1999.

9
00:00:47,290 --> 00:00:53,504
Then, in April 2025, after a terror attack in Pahalgam killed twenty-six people,

10
00:00:53,504 --> 00:00:58,330
India announced that the treaty would be held in abeyance.

11
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:06,239
The treaty divides the six rivers of the Indus basin.

12
00:01:06,239 --> 00:01:12,288
The three eastern rivers, the Ravi, the Beas and the Sutlej, are for India's unrestricted use.

13
00:01:12,288 --> 00:01:17,382
The three western rivers, the Indus, the Jhelum and the Chenab, go to Pakistan,

14
00:01:17,382 --> 00:01:22,776
though India may use them in limited ways, such as run-of-the-river hydropower.

15
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,215
The treaty also set up a Permanent Indus Commission, with one commissioner from each country,

16
00:01:31,215 --> 00:01:37,562
and a three-step way to settle disputes: the Commission, then a Neutral Expert, then a Court of Arbitration.

17
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:52,558
Since April 2025, India has flushed sediment from dams on the Chenab and sped up hydropower projects such as Pakal Dul and Ratle.

18
00:01:56,033 --> 00:02:02,196
Then, on the thirty-first of August 2026, a Court of Arbitration under the treaty issued an interim award.

19
00:02:02,196 --> 00:02:09,063
It said India's grounds were not enough to suspend the treaty, and it limited some construction at Ratle for now.

20
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:14,855
Here is where each side stands.

21
00:02:14,855 --> 00:02:22,277
India says the treaty stays in abeyance until Pakistan credibly and irrevocably abjures its support for cross-border terrorism.

22
00:02:22,277 --> 00:02:29,419
It says the court was set up in breach of the treaty and has no jurisdiction, and India has never appeared before it.

23
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:44,116
Pakistan says the award shows the treaty remains fully in force and binding, and that India must comply with it.

24
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:50,074
What is India's stake?

25
00:02:50,074 --> 00:02:55,520
First, security: India links the treaty to its fight against cross-border terrorism.

26
00:02:55,520 --> 00:03:01,544
Second, energy: fuller use of the western rivers means more hydropower for Jammu and Kashmir.

27
00:03:01,544 --> 00:03:10,045
Third, precedent: India is downstream of China on the Brahmaputra, so it has its own interest in how river agreements are read.

28
00:03:16,333 --> 00:03:18,766
Here is your passport stamp.

29
00:03:18,766 --> 00:03:22,624
East is R-B-S: Ravi, Beas, Sutlej, for India.

30
00:03:22,624 --> 00:03:26,985
West is I-J-C: Indus, Jhelum, Chenab, for Pakistan.

31
00:03:26,985 --> 00:03:32,904
And the year is nineteen sixty, with the World Bank as the broker.

32
00:03:39,333 --> 00:03:40,133
Exam desk.

33
00:03:40,133 --> 00:03:45,225
Statement one: the treaty gives India unrestricted use of the Chenab.

34
00:03:45,225 --> 00:03:45,734
False.

35
00:03:45,734 --> 00:03:52,798
The Chenab is a western river; India's unrestricted rights are on the Ravi, Beas and Sutlej.

36
00:03:55,567 --> 00:03:59,610
Statement two: the treaty was brokered by the World Bank.

37
00:03:59,610 --> 00:04:00,329
True.

38
00:04:03,167 --> 00:04:10,763
In Mains, you might be asked whether water can be an instrument of foreign policy, with reference to the Indus Waters Treaty.

39
00:04:10,763 --> 00:04:17,213
Answer in three parts: the treaty and its record, India's security argument and Pakistan's legal argument,

40
00:04:17,213 --> 00:04:21,010
and the options ahead, from renegotiation to restoration.

41
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:32,225
Three things to watch: whether the Permanent Indus Commission meets again, India's hydropower projects on the Chenab as they come online,

42
00:04:32,225 --> 00:04:39,424
and how both sides respond to the award, ahead of the Neutral Expert's decision on Ratle, expected in July 2027.

43
00:04:44,667 --> 00:04:51,414
On the relations web, a new thread joins India and Pakistan: Indus Waters, in abeyance.

44
00:04:51,414 --> 00:04:55,547
Next briefing: India and Pakistan after Pahalgam.

